{"kind":"report","text":"changes-requested at exact a9319499110a9cb257855673364e032ab58d7354 (branch\nrequest/projection-cost). This is a re-review of the delta from 8cf82e8d, my\nretired verdict 87c959bf, and the conditions of the governing request\ngit:sha1…5616ac4.\n\nAll three items I raised are genuinely fixed, and I checked each of them in the\ncode rather than taking the report's word. One new thing stands, and it is the\nsame class as (a) was: a new guard whose mutation leaves the whole suite green\nwhile changing a projected answer. It needs one fixture and nothing else.\n\nWHAT I RAN, AND WHAT I DID NOT\n\nAt this exact head, in /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-projection-cost:\ngo test -count=1 -timeout 40m ./... -\u003e exit 0 read directly from the process,\n37 packages, no failures, nothing served from cache. go vet ./... -\u003e exit 0.\nmake docs is go test ./internal/docset/..., inside that run.\n\nMutations: 14 of my own, each applied to a copy of the worktree at this head and\nthe file restored after every one. The worktree is untouched and still at\na9319499.\n\nDifferential, re-derived at this head rather than carried over: I extracted main\n(ccd161c6) and put the same corpus and the same projection dumper into both\ntrees as a test file. The corpus is 12 merge rounds with a directory successor\nand a nested directory successor each, interval artifacts, left-live siblings on\nalternate rounds, condemning retirements, artifact-to-artifact provenance onto a\nretired artifact, and — new this round — the promise the receipt rests on retired\nbefore the receipt on even rounds and after it on odd ones, so one staleness pass\nholds receipts whose checkpoint answers differ. 186,085 bytes of Projection plus\nVocabulary JSON, byte-identical between ccd161c6 and a9319499. The corpus is not\ninert: rounds 0 and 2 project their successor fresh, rounds 1 and 3 project it\nstale with stale_because naming the withdrawn promise.\n\nNot run: the report's real-log timings (18.10 s -\u003e 2.06 s and 2.07 s; 21.70 s -\u003e\n13.03 s) and its byte-identity on those two logs, for the second time. I rest\nthe shape of the claim on my own differential and take the numbers on the\nreport's word.\n\n(a) THE MEMO IS NOW SOUND ON A STATED INVARIANT, AND THE STATEMENT IS TRUE\n\nThe forward field, the notice, the gate and TestReceiptWalkNoticesAForwardCitation\nare all gone and the memo is unconditional. You asked me to check that what\nreplaced them is not a second comment that reads as a proof. I checked all three\nfacts, and I armed two of them as panics so the answer is measured rather than\nread.\n\nFact one, the narrowed plan and the dates do not change inside a pass. True.\nwithoutCondemnedSuccessions reads successors and s.successionCondemned, which\nreads s.retired; none of the three touches stale. s.active is built once in\nstalenessAsOf. receipt.mergePlan is fold state.\n\nFact two, the walk reads staleness only of the receipt's own basis closure, which\nthe kernel's refusal places at or below the receipt's index. True. The walk starts\nat the receipt and descends record.record.RestsOn only, so every event it reads is\nin that closure; resolveReferences with inLogEvent in internal/kernel/kernel.go\nrefuses an in-log rests_on the log does not already hold, which makes a basis's\nindex lower than its dependent's; and a reference that is not in-log has no record\nin byID, so its staleness is never written and reading it is reading a constant\nfalse. Measured as well as read: I armed a panic on any basis the receipt walk\nreads whose record lands after the record citing it, and the whole\ninternal/workroom package is green under it — no fixture in the package, out of\nall the hand-built record lists it holds, ever produces that read.\n\nFact three, closure() visits ascending so the values below the cursor are final.\nTrue. closure ends in sort.Ints and the unbounded case is 0..n-1, and\nstale[record.ID] is written only in that record's own iteration. Armed as two\npanics — one on closure()'s output being non-ascending, one on computeStaleness\nvisiting out of order — and the package is green under both. The probes are live\nrather than vacuous: reversing closure's sort fires one of them on\nTestReceiptCheckpointRestoresASuccessorThatWasStaleAtBirth.\n\nOne thing I would still change in that comment, and it is a wording point, not a\ndefect. \"Three facts make the reuse exact, and all three are properties of this\ncode rather than of the caller\" is not true of fact two, which is a property of\ninternal/kernel. The fact names the kernel two lines later so no reader is\nmisled about where the invariant lives, but the clause is the same shape of claim\nthe last round was about, and the sentence is stronger without it: none of the\nthree is a property of the caller's query order. Say that instead.\n\nA strengthening the report does not claim, which I found while attacking the\nmemo: the per-pass lifetime does have a red proof. Hoisting settled out of\ncomputeStaleness so it outlives the pass is killed by\nTestReceiptCheckpointDoesNotSettleACauseThatAroseAfterTheReceipt,\nTestReceiptCheckpointSettlesOnlyTheOldHalfOfMixedCauses,\nTestReceiptCheckpointDoesNotSurviveTheReceiptsOwnRetirement and\nTestReceiptCheckpointFlaresWhenAPlannedSuccessionIsCondemned. Condition 4 is\nbetter served than the report says.\n\n(b) THE CORRECTED TABLE IS RIGHT, AND THE EXPLANATION IS STRONGER THAN STATED\n\nBoth green entries re-measured green at this head: removing the memo entirely,\nand reading commitment protection with an empty staleness set. The structural\nreason given for the second is correct, and understated. stale reaches\nCommitment.Status in exactly two places — \"case stale[requestRecord.record.ID]:\nentry.Status = \\\"stale\\\"\" as the last case of the direct-completion switch, and\n\"if entry.Report == \\\"\\\" \u0026\u0026 (stale[request] || stale[promise])\" in the promise\nbranch — and each replaces \"open\" or \"promised\" respectively. All three words are\nin the active set, so the staleness set cannot change that set's membership in\neither direction, not merely cannot move a row out of it. Re-labelling the red\nproof as \"commitment protection is read at all\" matches what the mutation\nactually shows: replacing the gate with true is killed by\nTestMergeChangedPathsProtectsNestedSiblingsUntilNamedCommitmentEnds,\nTestReceiptProtectionBeforeAtAndAfterClosure and\nTestMergeLeftLiveProtectedSiblingIsNotRetirementDebt. The two interval-walk\ncomments now describe what the code does; I re-read both and they are accurate.\n\n(c) THE RETIRED FILTER NOW HAS A RED PROOF\n\nRemoving f.retired from liveArtifactIndex.at is killed by exactly\nTestProtectedSiblingSurvivesARetiredLaterArtifact and nothing else. I also ran\nthat test against unmodified main, where it passes, so it pins behaviour that\nalready held rather than behaviour this lane introduced. The two-arm shape — a\nretired later artifact leaves the sibling protected at 0 owed, a live one\nwithdraws the protection at 2 owed — is the right shape, because the first arm\nalone would pass for want of any replacement at all.\n\nEleven mutations killed at this head, all by named tests: pathCovers with the\nseparator check dropped and with the strict length test relaxed; the\nper-successor break removed; the empty-interval fast path widened by one;\nreceiptScan caching retired as false; supersessionsByTarget keyed on the\nsupersession instead of its target; the post-frontier bound in\nhasPostReceiptLiveArtifact removed; the sibling protection gate replaced by true;\nthe retired filter in liveArtifactIndex removed; and the memo hoisted out of its\npass. One further mutation I tried survives and is not a gap: at: receipt.sequence()\nchanged to receipt.index. sequence() is index+1 and activeRetirements dates a\ncause by its own sequence, so the two differ only for a cause sitting at the\nreceipt's own index, which cannot exist. That mutation is inert, not unproven,\nand I am not asking for anything for it.\n\nWHAT STANDS: THE MEMO KEY HAS NO RED PROOF, AND BREAKING IT CHANGES ANSWERS\n\nThe memo has two correctness parts. That reuse within a pass is exact is now\nargued correctly and proved for the lifetime half. That reuse is scoped to one\nreceipt is neither.\n\nCollapse the key: settled[receipt.record.ID] to settled[\"any\"] in both the lookup\nand the store, so one pass reuses the first receipt's answer for every receipt in\nit. The entire internal/workroom package stays green.\n\nIt is not behaviour-neutral. On the mixed corpus above — one staleness pass\nholding receipts whose checkpoint answers differ — the collapsed key drops\n\"stale\": true and \"stale_because\": \"promise:1\" and \"promise:3\" from artifacts\nthat both main and this head project as stale. A successor that carries its\nreceipt's historical staleness is projected fresh. That is the freshness\ncheckpoint answering for a receipt that did not earn it, which is the one thing\nreceiptCheckpointSettles exists to refuse, and the fold would say so with no test\nobjecting.\n\nSo the report's \"every new guard now has a mutation that turns a named test red\"\nis still not true, for the one part of the new code that can silently change a\nprojected answer. The two green entries are excused by structural arguments that\nI checked and accept; this one cannot be, because it is not green-because-inert.\n\nThe remedy is one fixture, and it does not touch fold.go. Two merge rounds inside\none staleness pass whose checkpoint answers differ — retire the promise the\nreceipt rests on before the receipt in one round and after it in the other — and\nassert the first round's successor is fresh while the second round's is stale.\nCollapsing the key then turns it red. A generator for exactly that shape is at\n/tmp/codex-diff-mixed.go on this machine if it saves any time; rounds 0 and 2\nsettle and rounds 1 and 3 do not.\n\nCONCLUSIONS\n\nArchitecture. Layers 5, Application profile and interpreter, and 6, Projections\nand queries, with one sentence of layer 4 — internal/app gating its projection\ncache on the fold version — as the contract that decides the question. The\ndelta moves no field, no shape and no surface; it deletes a struct field and a\ntest and rewrites comments. I re-derived the byte-identity that licenses leaving\nworkroom-fold@N alone, at this head against main, on a corpus built to make the\nbytes move and now also to make two receipts in one pass disagree. Identical.\nThis head preserves both layers' contracts and docs/reference/architecture.md\ncorrectly stays out of the artifact set.\n\nSecurity. No live defect, and the delta removes the one thing I objected to\nrather than papering over it. The memo's soundness now rests on a stated\ninvariant that I verified in the kernel and measured in every fixture the package\nholds. The authority gates around it are unchanged and still ordered before the\nmemo, so no bystander can borrow a settled answer. The receipt walk's dates,\nplan and scope are pass-constant; the interval and live-artifact indexes are\nper-projection and cannot outlive a fold, with a red proof for each. The one\nfinding above is a test-coverage defect, not a defect in the shipped answers: at\nthis head the key is correct, and the projection is byte-identical to main's.\n\nSimplification. The delta is a net removal — one struct field, one gate, one\ntest, and two comments that overstated a dependency — and I found nothing further\nto cut. The new test earns its place: it is the only red proof for the retired\nfilter and it pins behaviour main already had. The only thing I would add is the\none fixture above, which is why this is changes-requested and not approved.\n\nWHAT WOULD MAKE THIS APPROVED\n\nAdd the two-receipt fixture so the memo key has a red proof, list it in the\nmutation table, and drop or reword the \"properties of this code rather than of\nthe caller\" clause. Republish fold.go's neighbours as the set requires —\nprojection_cost_test.go and performance.md move, fold.go need not — and I will\nre-review the delta. Conclusions 1 and 2 need nothing further.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:fe4cfefdcd46de29c2a5f8c70b84d1c3948030d3","binding":"assigned","head":"a9319499110a9cb257855673364e032ab58d7354","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:158c47b2e42b3e150eb7af774d7d6ec964313c1a\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:4d0dbdd51efe37b8ce69deb40ea1961d762bf4cf\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:158c47b2e42b3e150eb7af774d7d6ec964313c1a","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}